WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.442 --> 00:00:03.181
- [Narrator] This is Charles,

00:00:03.181 --> 00:00:05.705
a San Esteban Island chuckwalla.

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:09.364
(lively, bouncy music)

00:00:09.364 --> 00:00:12.411
Part of the iguana family,
chuckwallas are the largest

00:00:12.411 --> 00:00:14.423
of the five species.

00:00:14.423 --> 00:00:17.451
They are endangered due to
hunting and feral animals

00:00:17.451 --> 00:00:19.732
preying on their eggs.

00:00:19.732 --> 00:00:22.647
Charles is native to San Esteban Island,

00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:26.663
a small island in the Gulf
of California, Mexico.

00:00:26.663 --> 00:00:29.221
It is only in this region
where the San Esteban

00:00:29.221 --> 00:00:31.791
Chuckwallas can be found.

00:00:31.791 --> 00:00:33.458
Their coloration provides critical

00:00:33.458 --> 00:00:35.987
camouflage from predators.

00:00:35.987 --> 00:00:38.355
They don't drink water, instead,

00:00:38.355 --> 00:00:42.110
getting all the water they
need from the plants they eat.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.277
San Esteban chuckwallas can
reach over 2.5 feet in size.

00:00:47.248 --> 00:00:50.001
They are very well adapted for desert life

00:00:50.001 --> 00:00:52.482
and enjoy basking in
the sun in temperatures

00:00:52.482 --> 00:00:55.918
consistently in the low hundreds.

00:00:55.918 --> 00:00:58.835
This is the San Esteban chuckwalla.

00:01:04.591 --> 00:01:07.174
(chiming beat)

